==> omaddons skip -h <==
Pass over one release; updates resume at the next

Usage: omaddons skip [OPTIONS] <ADDON>...

Arguments:
  <ADDON>...  The installed addons: each by its name, or a catalog name or alias

Options:
      --release <RELEASE-ID>  Pass over exactly this release, by its id from outdated or list -v
      --revision <N>          With --release: the release's source revision (WowAce)
      --version <VERSION>     Pass over every release whose version reads this (a label mark)
      --back                  Go back to the release before, and pass over the one it leaves
      --tx <TX>               With --back: go back from this transaction, as errors suggests
      --to <VERSION>          With --back: install this earlier version, as its source serves it now

Examples:
  omaddons skip plater -t retail                pass over the release waiting now
  omaddons skip dbm --version 12.2.0 -t retail  every release labelled 12.2.0
  omaddons skip dbm --back -t retail            go back to the files installed before

Global: -t <TARGET>  --json  --dry-run  -v  --quiet  --no-color  --offline  (--help lists them all)
==> omaddons skip --help <==
Pass over one release; updates resume at the next

Marks the release waiting for each addon, from what earlier checks remembered: a records-only change
that makes no request, undoable.

Usage: omaddons skip [OPTIONS] <ADDON>...

Arguments:
  <ADDON>...
          The installed addons: each by its name, or a catalog name or alias

Options:
      --release <RELEASE-ID>
          Pass over exactly this release, by its id from outdated or list -v
          
          gh:Owner/Repo@v6.2.5, whatever the release memo offers now. Takes one addon.

      --revision <N>
          With --release: the release's source revision (WowAce)
          
          Without it, a source that numbers revisions takes the release memo's revision of that
          release, else the mark holds every revision of the tag.

      --version <VERSION>
          Pass over every release whose version reads this (a label mark)
          
          For a release announced but not seen yet. Takes one addon.

      --back
          Go back to the release before, and pass over the one it leaves
          
          The very files installed then, from the local cache or held to their recorded hash; it
          never falls back to a label. Takes one addon.

      --tx <TX>
          With --back: go back from this transaction, as errors suggests
          
          Its full id, as history gives it. Without it, the transaction that installed the current
          release.

      --to <VERSION>
          With --back: install this earlier version, as its source serves it now
          
          Not promised to be the files once installed under that label. Not for url: or file: refs.

Examples:
  omaddons skip Plater -t retail                          pass over the release waiting now
  omaddons skip Plater --release gh:Tercioo/Plater-Nameplates@Plater-v662 -t retail
                                                          pass over exactly this release
  omaddons skip DBM --version 12.2.0 -t retail            every release labelled 12.2.0

A mark holds until unskip clears it, an install names that version, or the addon
moves to another source. update installs the next release as usual.

See also: unskip, outdated, update, undo

Global options:
      --json              One JSON envelope on stdout
      --format <FORMAT>   Output format [possible values: json, human]
  -t, --target <TARGET>   The client to act on: id, game mode, family, path, or all [alias:
                          --flavor]
      --dry-run           Show the plan without changing anything
      --actor <ACTOR>     Who is acting: human, agent or widget [env: OMADDONS_ACTOR]
      --reason <TEXT>     Why, recorded verbatim in history
      --offline           Fail any network access [env: OMADDONS_OFFLINE=1]
      --while-running     Allow folder changes while the client runs (never SavedVariables)
      --if-match <TOKEN>  Run only what the preview that returned this token showed
      --no-color          No ANSI color
      --no-progress       No progress output
      --progress <MODE>   Progress events on stderr [possible values: json, human, none]
      --quiet             Only errors on stderr
  -v, --verbose...        More detail (repeatable)
  -h, --help              Show help

Learn more: omaddons robot-docs guide
